Title: THE TELL-TALE HEART
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from the short story by Edgar Allan Poe
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. CELL - NIGHT

A dark, stone-walled cell illuminated only by a narrow beam of moonlight from a high barred window. NARRATOR (30s, pale, dark wild eyes, unkempt hair, threadbare dark wool coat) sits motionless on a wooden chair, staring directly forward into the camera.

(SOUND: distant wind howling through stone gaps, slow dripping water)

NARRATOR
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad? The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute. I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad? Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

(SOUND: water drops hitting damp stone floor)

NARRATOR
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night. Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult. For his gold I had no desire. I think it was his eye! yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it. Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Pitch-black interior. OLD MAN (70s, gaunt, wispy white hair, nightshirt) lies sleeping deeply beneath heavy quilts. The heavy oak door unlatches without a sound. NARRATOR slips through the gap, holding a brass dark lantern with its shutter fully closed.

(SOUND: low door hinge creaking, heavy rhythmic breathing of sleeper)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently! And then, when I had made an opening sufficient for my head, I put in a dark lantern, all closed, closed, that no light shone out, and then I thrust in my head.

NARRATOR carefully unfastens the lantern shutter a fraction of an inch. A single pencil-thin ray of light shoots across the dark room, casting a pinprick beam onto the sleeping man's closed eye.

(SOUND: subtle metallic latch slide)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work; for it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - DAY

Bright morning light streams through open wooden shutters. OLD MAN sits propped up in bed with a tray resting across his lap. NARRATOR stands beside the bed holding a steaming porcelain teapot, smiling warmly.

(SOUND: birds chirping outside, tea pouring into a china cup)

NARRATOR
Good morning! How have you passed the night, my dear friend?

OLD MAN smiles gently, nodding, accepting the cup with trembling hands.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Total darkness. The door inches open in absolute silence. NARRATOR creeps into the room, holding the dark lantern in front of him. His thumb slides across the tin fastener.

(SOUND: sharp tin latch click echoing loudly in the silent room)

OLD MAN bolts upright in bed, peering blind into the blackness.

(SOUND: bedsprings creaking sharply)

OLD MAN
Who's there?

NARRATOR freezes solid in the doorway, lantern held perfectly motionless. He does not move a muscle. OLD MAN remains sitting straight up, listening in the pitch dark.

(SOUND: stifled groan of mortal terror from the bed, wind in the chimney)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Presently I heard a slight groan, and I knew it was the groan of mortal terror. It was not a groan of pain or of grief—oh, no!—it was the low stifled sound that arises from the bottom of the soul when overcharged with awe.

NARRATOR slowly slides open a tiny crevice in the lantern casing. A single thread of light shoots out and lands directly on OLD MAN's face.

The pale blue eye is wide open, staring unblinking into the light, covered by its milky film.

(SOUND: low, dull, rapid ticking sound like a watch wrapped in cotton)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And have I not told you that what you mistake for madness is but over-acuteness of the sense?—now, I say, there came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man's heart.

(SOUND: heartbeat quickens and grows louder, THUMP-thump, THUMP-thump)

(MUSIC: tense low cello drone building under the heartbeat)

NARRATOR's jaw clenches in fury as he stares at the unblinking eye. His breath comes in fast, silent gasped bursts.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It grew quicker and quicker, and louder and louder every instant. The old man's terror must have been extreme! It grew louder, I say, louder every moment!

NARRATOR throws open the lantern full wide, lets out a piercing yell, and leaps into the room.

OLD MAN
Ah!

NARRATOR violently drags OLD MAN off the mattress onto the hardwood floor and hauls the heavy wooden bedframe over him, slamming it down flat to smother him.

(SOUND: heavy timber crash, muffled thrashing against wooden floorboards)

(SOUND: muffled thumping heartbeat beneath floor slowing down... slower... stopping)

NARRATOR sits atop the wooden bedframe, chest heaving, a wild smile spreading across his face. He lifts the bedframe aside and presses his palm to OLD MAN's chest.

(SOUND: absolute silence, ticking clock outside in hallway)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was stone, stone dead. I placed my hand upon the heart and held it there many minutes. There was no pulsation. He was stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

NARRATOR kneels on the floor beside a large wooden wash tub. Using a crowbar, he carefully prys up three heavy floorboards. He lowers the wrapped remains beneath the joists and lays the planks smoothly back into place.

(SOUND: dull wooden thud of floorboards setting, liquid sloshing in tub)

NARRATOR rubs his hand over the seamless join in the floor. Not a mark or spot remains.

(SOUND: distant church bell chiming four heavy beats)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When I had made an end of these labors, it was four o'clock—still dark as midnight. As the bell sounded the hour, there came a knocking at the street door.

INT. HALLWAY AND FOYER - NIGHT

NARRATOR walks lightly down the front staircase holding a flickering candle. He approaches the heavy entry door, smiling confidently.

(SOUND: loud, steady knocking on front door panel)

NARRATOR unlatches the door and swings it open. Three police officers in dark Victorian coats and tall caps stand on the stoop: OFFICER REYNOLDS (40s, thick mustache, authoritative), OFFICER MALONE (30s, lean, observant), and OFFICER CHEN (30s, quiet, watchful).

(SOUND: night wind blowing into foyer)

OFFICER REYNOLDS
Good evening, sir. A neighbor reported a shriek heard from the house during the night. Suspicion of foul play was aroused, and we have been deputed to search the premises.

NARRATOR
Welcome, gentlemen! Welcome! The shriek was my own—a dream, I assure you, in my sleep. The old man is absent in the country. Pray, step inside.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - DAWN

First light of dawn streams through the shutter slits. NARRATOR opens drawers and wardrobe doors for OFFICER REYNOLDS, OFFICER MALONE, and OFFICER CHEN, showing undisturbed belongings.

(SOUND: heavy boots stepping across floorboards, soft rustle of wool coats)

NARRATOR brings four wooden chairs into the center of the room. He places three chairs for the officers and sets his own chair directly over the floorboards concealing the corpse.

NARRATOR
Rest from your fatigues, gentlemen! Here, sit and be at ease.

The three officers sit down comfortably, taking off their caps and setting them on their knees.

OFFICER REYNOLDS
Thank you, sir. All appears quite in order.

(SOUND: chair legs scraping on wood, pleasant murmured chatter among officers)

NARRATOR sits back, smiling brightly, answering their questions with quick, fluid gestures.

(SOUND: subtle low ringing tone beginning in ears)

NARRATOR's smile stiffens. He grips the armrests of his chair tight.

(SOUND: low, dull, quick sound rising from under the floor—thud-thud, thud-thud)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears: but still they sat and still chatted. The ringing became more distinct:—it continued and became more distinct: I talked more freely to get rid of the feeling: but it continued and gained definiteness—until, at length, I found that the noise was not within my ears.

OFFICER MALONE chuckles softly at a casual remark from OFFICER CHEN. The officers continue chatting comfortably.

(SOUND: heartbeat under floor growing louder, faster: THUMP-thump, THUMP-thump)

(MUSIC: high screeching violin dissonance rising in pitch)

NARRATOR stands up violently, pushing his chair back. He paces the floor back and forth with heavy, forceful strides, flailing his arms.

NARRATOR
A lovely morning, is it not, gentlemen? Most pleasant! Most agreeable!

OFFICER REYNOLDS looks up, smiling politely, gesturing with his hand as he continues speaking.

(SOUND: deafening beating heartbeat thumping through the entire room)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Why would they not be gone? I paced the floor to and fro with heavy strides, as if excited to fury by the observations of the men—but the noise steadily increased. Oh God! what could I do? I foamed—I raved—I swore!

NARRATOR grabs his chair, lifts it high over his head, and smashes it violently onto the floorboards.

(SOUND: wood splintering crash, roar of thumping heart at maximum volume)

The three officers rise slowly from their seats, looking at NARRATOR with calm, pleasant expressions, still smiling.

NARRATOR clutches his head with both hands, his face pale, eyes bulging, sweat pouring down his forehead. He points frantically down at the boards beneath his feet.

NARRATOR
Villains! Dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

INT. CELL - NIGHT

Back inside the dark stone cell. NARRATOR stands pressed back against the cold stone wall, staring wildly straight into the camera, panting heavily.

(SOUND: wind howling through barred window, muffled beating heart fading slowly into silence)

NARRATOR
It is the beating of his hideous heart!

NARRATOR slides down the stone wall into the deep shadows on the floor.

(SOUND: iron door bolt sliding shut with a heavy clank, total silence)

FADE OUT.
